  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY NIGHT THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 135 PM MDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\nOn Saturday, upper low moves into wrn WY, with strengthening wly\nflow over CO bringing in a drier air mass, especially over the\nmountains. While we won't shut off convection completely as\nupper trough extending south from the WY low will provide some\nmodest upward motion, storm coverage/strength will be lower \nthan the past few days over most of the higher terrain Sat\nafternoon and evening. For the eastern mountains and southeast\nplains, forecast hinges on how fast cold front arrives and how \nmuch low level instability develops behind it by late day. Most \nmodel forecasts have boundary near the Arkansas River by late \nSat afternoon, then progressing south toward the NM border \nthrough the evening. Combination of best upper forcing and \ngreatest low level instability are currently forecast over nern \nCO Sat afternoon/evening, though instability/shear over sern CO \nlook more than sufficient for storms to build s-sw into the \narea, especially mid to late evening. Nudged up NBM pops a bit \nSat afternoon/evening to account for this scenario, though \noverall blended guidance didn't look too bad for this period. \n\nStrong wly flow aloft on Sun will dry out the mountains, with \nconvection limited to the ern slopes of the Sangres/Wets/Pikes\nPeak. For the I-25 corridor and the sern plains, post frontal\nupslope will persist, keeping dewpoints in the 50s east of I-25\nthrough the day. 0-6km shear of 35-45 kts will be in place, \nwith models showing CAPE in the 1k-2k J/KG range from afternoon\ninto the evening, while 70-80 kt jet streak at 250 moves \noverhead late in the day. Set-up looks favorable for \nstrong/severe storms east of the mountains, though outflow and \npotential cloud cover from Sat night/early Sun morning storms \ncould limit instability, especially into early/mid afternoon. \nCurrent forecast has mainly isolated pops over the plains Sun \nafternoon, then widespread storms along/east of I-25 Sun evening\nas activity initially along the Palmer intensifies and expands \nsouth and east. Max temps behind the front on Sun will run 5-10 \ndegf cooler than Sat, while mountains see little change. \n\nLow confidence forecast for Mon-Wed as model solutions have\nbounced around a good deal, though rough consensus appears to \nfavor a return of low/mid level moisture to much of the area as \nwe approach mid-week, with western U.S. upper trough/sern U.S. \nupper ridge pattern potentially reforming. Blended model \nguidance shows cooler than average temperatures and daily \nchances for showers/thunderstorms, which looks fine for now.",
